  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1029 PM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nISOLATED FLASH FLOOD AND HAIL CONCERNS FRIDAY:\n\nSouthwesterly flow around the western edge of a broad high \npressure system continues Friday and through the weekend. Though\nThursday marked the expected peak in moisture, this slowly-\nchanging pattern will maintain daily chances of precipitation \nfor the foreseeable future, though storm impacts are anticipated\nto diminish.\n\nThough the last two days saw appreciable midday instability for\nthis region of the country, storms have often been unable to \nmaintain strength for long durations due to lacking shear and \ndense clouds quickly filling in during afternoons. The story \ncould be similar Friday. At this moment, models are consistent \nwith each other in lesser cloud cover existing Friday morning \nover the high terrain near the Divide, with denser cloud cover \nresiding in eastern Utah. This should leave the door open for \nanother day of increasing CAPE through the late morning over \nmuch of western Colorado. Also working in the storms' favor is \nthe potential for marginally increased shear as a mild jet \nstreak works its way through the southwesterly flow. On the \nother hand, moisture is on the decline, as mentioned before, \nwith PWAT's falling below 150% of normal for much of the region.\nAll things considered, most stronger storms capable of small \nhail and localized heavy rainfall (or the flash flooding \nconcerns that come with it) are expected to stay confined to \nColorado's high terrain. Unless areas near or west of the CO-UT \nborder see mostly sunny morning skies Friday, the likely storm \nmode for these regions will be stratiform rain showers. These \nstorms would still be capable of producing wetting rains while \nminimizing hydrologic concerns, but a stray stronger storm or \ntwo should not be ruled out just yet. All vulnerable areas \n(especially including burn scars) across the entire CWA will \nstill be at risk of flash flooding or debris flows Friday in \nspite of the more localized nature of storm coverage.\n\nWET WEATHER PERSISTS:\n\nEven as moisture returns to near-normal through the weekend and\nearly next week, southwesterly flow will support daily chances \nof precipitation focused around the mountains. Lacking upper-\nlevel support through the mid-week will likely mean storm \nimpacts will be on the milder side, but keep up-to-date over the\ncoming days for more details. Model guidance diverges \nsignificantly by Wednesday, but there is some potential for an \nuptick in activity late next week as a shortwave develops over \nthe western CONUS. Without model agreement, uncertainty leaves \nthis potential system as nothing more than an interesting \npossibility on the horizon, and any speculation on details will \nhave to wait until we see model guidance converge. Otherwise,\nlook for temperatures to begin to rise again with highs expected\nto return to ~5 degrees above normal by the middle of next week.",
